1. Cockpit is a web-based GUI that allows Linux administrators to monitor and manage Linux servers through a browser.

2. Real-Life Example

Imagine you have two ways to control your TV.

Method 1

Walk to the TV and press the buttons.

Similar to using the Linux Terminal.

Method 2

Use a remote control.

Similar to using Cockpit.

Both do the same work, but Cockpit provides a graphical interface.

3. Why Do We Need Cockpit?

Normally, to check CPU usage, memory usage, services, or logs, you run commands such as:

top
free -h
systemctl status ssh
journalctl

With Cockpit, you can view this information on a web page with graphs and menus.

4. How Cockpit Works
Administrator
       │
       ▼
Web Browser
       │
https://server-ip:9090
       │
       ▼
Cockpit Service
       │
       ▼
Linux Server

You open a browser, connect to the server, and manage it remotely.

5. Default Port

Cockpit uses:

9090

6. Features of Cockpit

Cockpit allows you to:

Monitor CPU usage
Monitor RAM usage
Monitor disk usage
View system logs
Manage users
Start, stop, and restart services
Monitor network interfaces
Manage storage
Manage virtual machines (with additional plugins)
Open a terminal in the browser

7.Installing Cockpit
Ubuntu
sudo apt update
sudo apt install cockpit

RHEL / Rocky / AlmaLinux
sudo dnf install cockpit

8.Start the Cockpit Service
sudo systemctl start cockpit

9.Enable Cockpit at Boot
sudo systemctl enable cockpit

Check Service Status
sudo systemctl status cockpit

Example Output

Active: active (running)

10.Allow Port 9090 (Firewall)

On systems using firewalld:

sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=cockpit --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

11. Access Cockpit

Open your browser and enter:

https://server-ip:9090

Example:

https://192.168.1.10:9090

You may see a browser warning because Cockpit often uses a self-signed certificate by default. You can proceed if you trust the server.

12. Login

Use your Linux username and password.

Example:

Username: bilal
Password: ********

After logging in, you can manage the server.
